# SurfContext tools — ARDS v5.2

The reference implementation of the standard: everything a project needs to make
its rules **pushed context** rather than discovered context, and to make the
push verifiable.

Licence: the scripts and templates in `tools/` and `templates/` are MIT; the
prose in `tools/README.surf` and `playbooks/` is CC-BY-SA 4.0. Both are the work
of CloudSurf Software LLC.

## The one contract everything shares

`CONTEXT.surf` holds a section whose heading line is exactly `## Core`. The
**Core body** is the bytes after the newline that terminates that line, up to but
excluding the first later line starting `## `. The **stamp** is the sha256 of
exactly those bytes, truncated to 16 hex characters, rendered as:

```
<!-- ards-core: sha256:XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX, ards v5.2 -->
```

Four implementations must agree byte-for-byte: the awk block in
`surfcontext-sync.sh`, the python twin in `ards-lint.py`, and the extractor in
each hook. A later `## Core Principles` heading must never match. The battery
checks the agreement on every run.

## Tools

| Tool | What it does |
|---|---|
| `surfcontext-sync.sh` | Generates `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` from the Core, stamped and idempotent, and materializes user-invocable skills into `.claude/commands/`. `--root <dir>` (default cwd), `--smoke`, `--quiet`. |
| `ards-lint.py` | Conformance linter. Fails on a missing, stale, or hand-edited block, an over-budget Core, an unmaterialized binding, and — in connector mode — a missing `workspace.key_env` or `.mcp.json`. `--root`, `--tier`, `--quiet`. |
| `make-connector.sh` | Generates a whole connector directory and refuses to finish unless it lints `guaranteed`. |
| `sync-skills.sh` | Fills the regenerable skill cache at `.context/skills/`. |
| `surfspace-mcp.py` | Stdlib-only JSON-RPC client for the workspace MCP server. Library calls return errors instead of raising, so a fail-open hook stays fail-open. |
| `transcript-tokens.py` | Sums harness-transcript tokens since the last marker, keyed on unique request id. |
| `task-flip.sh` | Flips one board card to a new stage carrying `stage_tokens`, then advances the marker. `--dry-run` posts nothing. |
| `hooks/session-start.sh` | SessionStart binding: receipt plus the workspace digest. |
| `hooks/stop.sh` | Stop binding: write-path reminder, token line, receipt. |
| `contamination-gate.sh` | The commit door for a public tree. |
| `ards-tests.sh` | The battery. Fixtures are built from scratch under `mktemp -d`. |

## The connector layout

`make-connector.sh <dir> --workspace-id <id> --workspace-name <name>
--key-env <VAR> --core <file> [--folder-id <id>] [--projects <file>] [--url <url>]`
emits:

```
<dir>/
  CONTEXT.surf            hand-authored ## Core — the ONLY prose that stays local
  CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md    GENERATED from the Core, stamped, lint-checked
  surfcontext.json        version 5.2, home surfspace, workspace + projects
  .mcp.json               project-scoped MCP entry — an env-var REFERENCE, never a key
  .claude/settings.json   SessionStart and Stop bindings
  .context/tools/         a vendored copy of this directory, so the connector stands alone
  .context/skills/        regenerable skill cache (gitignored)
  .gitignore              runtime state and the cache
```

A connector is not a library. Docs, plans and fragments live in the workspace and
are pulled fresh each session; duplicating them locally is what the linter warns
about in connector mode.

Every emitted file is compare-then-write and carries no timestamp, so a second
generation over the same inputs changes nothing on disk. `--core` is copied
verbatim and is rejected outright when it has no exact `## Core` heading line:
generating a connector around an unextractable Core would produce a directory
that can never reach `guaranteed`.

The vendored tree is stamped in `.context/tools/.vendor-stamp` with this
repository's commit sha, or with an explicit unknown marker when the source is
not a git checkout. Vendored files are overwritten on the next generation — edit
them here, never there.

## Hook contracts

Both hooks are **fail-open**: every step is wrapped, output is bounded, and the
exit status is always 0. A bug in a hook must never block a session.

**SessionStart** prints the receipt:

```
ards-receipt: v5.2 core sha256:<16 hex> tier:<guaranteed|injected|discovered> status:<verified|STALE-RUN-SYNC>
```

`status:verified` means the stamp in `CLAUDE.md` equals a freshly recomputed sha;
the tier is whatever `ards-lint.py --tier` measures, not a literal. In connector
mode it then appends a bounded workspace digest: important documents (capped at
eight, with the first two `answers:` lines when they are cheap to fetch), the
newest `active-work` documents, the open cards in `build` and `verify`, and a
repo roll-call over `projects[]` that WARNs when a repository expected on the
workspace remote is not on it. Ten seconds of wall clock, sixty lines of stdout,
both hard caps. The digest is cached at `.context/.digest-cache.json`; when the
workspace is unreachable the hook prints that cache behind a `STALE-CACHE` line
with its age, and when there is no cache it prints the `NO-DIGEST` line and the
session simply works offline from the Core.

The key is read from the environment variable **named** in `workspace.key_env`.
The name lives in the manifest; the key never does, and is never echoed, logged,
or written to the cache.

**Stop** prints the write-path reminder — worded for the workspace in connector
mode and for the local fragment stream otherwise — then one token line, then the
receipt again, so a Core edit made mid-session cannot leave the root files
silently stale. It reads its JSON payload on stdin for `transcript_path` and
`session_id`.

## Counting stage tokens

`transcript-tokens.py` reads the harness transcript, one JSON object per line,
and selects assistant entries recorded after the marker in
`.context/.token-marker.json`.

**The trap it exists for:** a streamed request appears as SEVERAL lines that
share one `requestId` and carry IDENTICAL usage. Summing per line multiplies the
bill. Every sum is therefore keyed on the unique request id, and each request is
counted exactly once. The transcript format is version-specific, so every field
is read defensively and an unrecognised shape is skipped rather than fatal.

The result is written to `.context/.session-tokens.json`; `task-flip.sh` posts it
as `stage_tokens` and only then advances the marker, so a failed post is retried
against the same window rather than silently losing it.

## The commit door

Two gates, both of which must pass before anything here is committed:

```bash
bash tools/contamination-gate.sh     # public-tree contamination
python3 tools/ards-lint.py           # conformance
bash tools/ards-tests.sh             # the battery (runs both of the above)
```

`contamination-gate.sh` greps `tools/`, `templates/` and `playbooks/` — or the
paths you name — for the classes defined in `tools/.contamination-terms`: tailnet
and local host forms, cloud resource ids, money amounts, absolute paths under a
user home or an external volume, harness transcript project keys, UUIDs (which is
how workspace, document and folder ids leak), card ids, third-party company
names, AI-attribution strings, and secrets by prefix. It exits 1 listing every
hit by file and line.

Site-specific rosters — machine nicknames, owner prefixes, internal code names —
are deliberately **absent** from this public repository. Supply them at run time:

```bash
bash tools/contamination-gate.sh --terms /path/to/private-roster
bash tools/contamination-gate.sh --allow /path/to/permitted-lines
```

Two self-exemptions are structural: the pattern definitions and any `--terms`
file describe the contamination rather than containing it, and would otherwise
report themselves on every run. The public product endpoint and the owner's own
domain are allowed explicitly, since the host classes would otherwise swallow
them.

## Skills

`sync-skills.sh --root <dir> [--from <dir>]` fills `.context/skills/<name>/SKILL.surf`.

It probes the MCP server ONCE with `tools/list` and caches the advertised roster
for the run. `list_skills` and `read_skill` **do not exist on the workspace
server yet**; the probe is there to find them absent and fall through silently to
the `--from` directory, copying every immediate-child `SKILL.surf` that opens
with YAML frontmatter, bytes preserved exactly. When the verbs ship, the same
command starts pulling from the workspace with no change here.

It prints one summary line naming the source (`mcp` or `local`) and the count,
and writes `.context/skills/.sync-manifest.json`. It **never deletes a file it
did not write**: a stale skill is a far smaller problem than a blanket wipe of a
directory someone put work in.

## Playbooks

`playbooks/` holds the generic, publishable session playbooks — `checkpoint`,
`create-surfdoc`, `task-breakdown`, `gather-context`, `machine-handoff` — in the
same `SKILL.surf` frontmatter contract the sync consumes. They are starting
points: copy one into a project's skill source and specialise it there.
